@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+Replace observations with percentile values
+===========================================
+
+The :class:`seaborn.objects.Perc` stat replaces observations with requested
+percentile values and records each level in a ``percentile`` column. Integer
+``k`` requests that many evenly spaced levels from 0 to 100, inclusive; a list
+specifies the levels directly. The default ``k=5`` produces quartiles and the
+endpoints.
+
+``method`` selects the percentile calculation used by NumPy. The default is
+``"linear"``. Choices are ``"inverted_cdf"``, ``"averaged_inverted_cdf"``,
+``"closest_observation"``, ``"interpolated_inverted_cdf"``, ``"hazen"``,
+``"weibull"``, ``"linear"``, ``"median_unbiased"``, ``"normal_unbiased"``,
+``"lower"``, ``"higher"``, ``"midpoint"``, and ``"nearest"``.
+
+Each group produces one row per requested percentile. Combine ``Perc`` with a
+range mark to draw an interval between selected percentile rows.
+
+Examples
+--------
+
+.. plot::
+
+    from seaborn import load_dataset
+    import seaborn.objects as so
+
+    penguins = load_dataset("penguins").dropna()
+
+    # Default quartiles and endpoints
+    so.Plot(penguins, x="species", y="body_mass_g").add(so.Dot(), so.Perc())
+
+    # Three evenly spaced levels
+    so.Plot(penguins, x="species", y="body_mass_g").add(so.Dot(), so.Perc(3))
+
+    # Explicit levels, with the 10th to 90th percentile range
+    (
+        so.Plot(penguins, x="species", y="body_mass_g")
+        .add(so.Range(), so.Perc([10, 90]))
+        .add(so.Dot(), so.Perc([50]))
+    )
